Local changes to the upstream files
-----------------------------------

1. resources/gem5_wrappers/SConscript was renamed to SConscript.wrapper.
   gem5's SConstruct runs every file named SConscript under ext/; the
   upstream file is a template meant to be copied into a gem5 source
   directory and stops the build (Exit(1)) when its RAMULATOR2_HOME
   placeholder is not edited.
2. .gitignore, line 3: "ext/" became "/ext/nanobind/", so that the
   vendored yaml-cpp and fmt below are tracked; the line was then removed,
   so that the vendored nanobind is tracked too.
3. src/ramulator/controller/CMakeLists.txt: the three files of gem5's
   address-mapping policies below, for upstream's standalone build;
   gem5's SConscript glob already covers them.
4. python/ramulator/addr_mapper/__init__.py: regenerated by the code
   generator for the new mappers.
5. tests/utils/test_harness.cpp: ControllerUnderTestCpp gains
   map_address(intra_channel_addr), which applies the controller's
   address mapper as ControllerBase::send does, and its nanobind
   binding; used by tests/unit_tests/test_gem5_policies.py. Test code
   only. Its command accounting also leaves out PIM commands (type_id
   2), which come from gem5's PimSequencer plugin, not from the harness.
6. src/ramulator/controller/controller_base.{h,cpp}: public members for
   gem5's PIM command sequencer, all inert by default: set_host_filter()
   (a filter on host reads and writes, applied in pick_rw_if()),
   request_write_drain() (while set and writes wait, set_write_mode()
   keeps write mode), the read-only views read_buffer_size(),
   write_buffer_size(), priority_buffer_size(), read_buffer_capacity(),
   write_buffer_capacity(), has_active_in() and peek_host(). With no
   filter and the drain off, scheduling is unchanged (an issued-command
   trace of the stock standards is identical).
7. src/ramulator/dram/dram_spec.h, device.h, device.cpp: a bank target
   BankTarget::Pair for gem5's single-bank PIM pair command: banks 2k
   and 2k + 1 of one bank group (the even bank named by the address).
   for_each_target_bank_while() visits both, issue_command() and
   check_timing() update and check the timing path of both, and a new
   helper pair_addr_vec() gives their addresses. Also
   ControllerBase::would_close_active() (controller_base.cpp) looks at
   both banks of a closing Pair command. No stock command has the Pair
   target, so nothing changes without PIM commands.
8. src/ramulator/controller/impl/lpddr_controller_base.{h,cpp} and
   lpddr6_controller.cpp: the four non-bank PIM commands (PIM_GB_RD,
   PIM_GB_WR, PIM_REG_RD, PIM_REG_WR) join is_access_cmd(), the two
   reads is_read_cmd(), so the controller syncs WCK before them; their
   ids are looked up only when the standard has them (LPDDR6_PIM),
   otherwise -1.
9. src/ramulator/controller/impl/hbm34_controller.cpp: ACTab joins
   is_all_bank_row_command(), and, as a 1.5-clock row command like ACT,
   gets ACT's three-tick falling-edge pairing window in
   record_rising_row_issued_command_info(). Id -1 without ACTab.
10. src/ramulator/controller/impl/gddr7_controller.cpp: the two non-bank
   PIM reads join is_read_like(), so RCK is started before them, and
   reads_waiting() also counts a read-like entry of the priority buffer,
   where those reads wait, so that rck_mode start_with_rckstrt sends
   RCKSTRT for them. That loop matches only the two PIM read ids
   (is_read_like() also matches the stock RD and RDA, which a test or a
   plugin may put in the priority buffer), so a stock entry changes
   nothing. Ids -1 without them.
11. src/ramulator/controller/refresh/impl/all_bank.cpp: four rows of
   all_bank_refresh_scopes for gem5's PIM standards (DDR5_PIM and
   LPDDR6_PIM Rank, GDDR7_PIM Channel, HBM4_PIM PseudoChannel; array
   size 11 to 15); all-bank refresh stops on an unknown standard name
   otherwise.
12. src/ramulator/dram/CMakeLists.txt: the PIM command headers and the
   four generated *_PIM.cpp files.
13. python/ramulator/dram/__init__.py: regenerated by the code generator
   for the four PIM standards; likewise
   python/ramulator/{memory_system,controller_plugin}/__init__.py for
   PimDRAM and PimSequencer.
14. src/ramulator/CMakeLists.txt (add_subdirectory(pim)) and
   src/ramulator/memory_system/CMakeLists.txt: the new pim/ directory
   and the PimDRAM memory system, for upstream's standalone build;
   gem5's SConscript glob already covers them. Both files were also
   converted from CRLF to LF line endings, so their diffs against the
   import show every line.
15. src/ramulator/memory_system/channel_mapper/impl/gem5_port_interleave.cpp:
   interleave_size is an Addr_t (int64_t), not an int, so that one
   channel's capacity (2 GiB and more), the interleave of the ChRaBaRoCo
   policy, fits. Values below 2^31 behave as before. Its generated
   wrapper python/ramulator/channel_mapper/gem5_port_interleave.py
   carries cpp_type="Addr_t" (regenerated 2026-11-10; code-generator
   metadata only, the Python value stays an int).

16. In python/ramulator/dram/pim.py the defaults of nCCD_AB and nCCDW_AB
   (the same-bank column gaps), nEX_AB_WR (write latency + burst) and
   nSB_RD_DONE (tRTP) of the four descriptors, and derive_pim's rule 2,
   which also pairs AB columns with SB columns and with AB columns of
   the other class through the Bank and BankGroup rows;
   resolve_pim_defaults() and a resolve() in
   python/ramulator/dram/{ddr5,lpddr6,gddr7,hbm4}_pim.py evaluate the
   PIM defaults after the host overrides unless the PIM value is given;
   src/ramulator/pim/pim_sequencer.cpp completes a non-bank load after
   nCL_x + (read_latency - host CAS latency), one clock more on GDDR7;
   test T15 in tests/device_timings/pim_cases.py. All gem5's own files;
   the generated sources are unchanged.
17. python/ramulator/dram/pim.py's descriptors also hold each PIM
   standard's preset (organization and timing presets, organization
   overrides, controller, default channel count) and its host timing
   overrides with their reasons, the one source that gem5's
   src/python/gem5/components/memory/pim.py (PIM_PRESETS),
   libpim/gen_pim_layout.py (STANDARDS) and libpim/timing read. The
   presets give 32 GiB PIM memories: DDR5 DDR5_16Gb_x8 with one rank x 4
   channels, LPDDR6 x 16, GDDR7 GDDR7_32Gb_x8 x 32 (refresh overrides
   nRFCab/nRFMab 473, nRFCpb/nRFMpb 106), HBM4 HBM4_32Gb_8Hi x 32;
   tests/device_timings/pim_cases.py (PRESETS, also used by
   tests/controller_scheduling) runs DDR5 with two ranks, so its cases
   keep a second unit per channel.
18. src/ramulator/controller/impl/lpddr_controller_base.{h,cpp} and
   lpddr6_controller.cpp: a bank between a PIM ACTsb1/ACTab1 and its
   ACT-2 (the Activating state without a host owner, pim_activating())
   holds back closing and refresh commands to it and any other ACT-1, as
   m_act2_owner_valid does for a host ACT-2 (would_block_activating,
   has_pending_pim_act2, is_allowed_during_pending_act2), and a PIM
   ACT-2 waiting in the priority buffer is issued ahead of the buffer's
   head, after an urgent host ACT-2 (pick_pim_act2), as host ACT-2s
   bypass it through the activating buffer. The ids and the state are
   looked up only when the standard has PIM commands (LPDDR6_PIM),
   otherwise -1: without PIM commands every Activating bank has a host
   owner and nothing changes.
19. python/ramulator/dram/pim.py's derive_pim adds rows for the data
   dependencies inside a unit (a column that writes the register file
   before a register read or write by its execution time, AB_REGBK
   before a register write, a column that reads the GB before a GB
   write, a GB write before such a column by nCWL_GB plus the host write
   burst, the descriptors' new bl), and DDR5's PREsb joins the PIM
   timing rows to PREs; tests T17, T18 in
   tests/device_timings/pim_cases.py and
   r14_gddr7_rck_counts_only_pim_reads in
   tests/controller_scheduling/pim_cases.py; the loop of change 10
   narrowed. gem5's own files apart from item 10; the generated sources
   are unchanged.

Comments in the changed upstream files name the change they belong to
("README.gem5 change N").

All later changes are separate commits on top of the commit that added
this directory; `git log -- ext/ramulator2` lists them.

Files added for gem5
--------------------

- src/ramulator/controller/addr_mapper/impl/gem5_addr_mapper.h,
  ro_ra_ba_ch_co.cpp (RoRaBaChCo, RoRaBaCoCh) and ro_co_ra_ba_ch.cpp
  (RoCoRaBaCh, parameter stripe_size): ports of gem5's three
  address-mapping policies (gem5 DRAMInterface::decodePacket,
  src/mem/dram_interface.cc:856-895), applied to the channel-local
  address that Gem5PortInterleave leaves. Least significant first: the
  column, gem5's flat bank (bank group, bank, stack ID) and its rank (Rank
  or PseudoChannel), the row; RoCoRaBaCh splits the column at gem5's
  channel stripe (the interleave with more than one channel, else one
  transaction, gem5 src/mem/mem_interface.cc:64-65). Their Python
  wrappers python/ramulator/addr_mapper/ro_ra_ba_ch_co.py,
  ro_ra_ba_co_ch.py and ro_co_ra_ba_ch.py are generated.
- gem5's processing-in-memory (PIM) standards:
  python/ramulator/dram/pim.py (the per-standard descriptors and
  derive_pim(), which builds the PIM rows from a deep copy of the stock
  rows by the rules in its docstring, so the stock standards are unchanged) and
  python/ramulator/dram/{ddr5,lpddr6,gddr7,hbm4}_pim.py (DDR5_PIM,
  LPDDR6_PIM, GDDR7_PIM, HBM4_PIM, subclasses of the stock standards as
  DDR5_VRR); their generated src/ramulator/dram/impl/*_PIM.cpp; the
  command headers src/ramulator/dram/commands/{PIM_*,ACTab*,ACTsb*}.h
  (direct commands: no preq, not is_accessing or is_opening); the tests
  tests/device_timings/pim_cases.py and test_pim_{ddr5,lpddr6,gddr7,
  hbm4}.py (the earliest-legal-cycle tests T1-T18).
- gem5's PIM memory system and command sequencer:
  src/ramulator/memory_system/i_pim_memory_system.h (the interfaces
  IPimMemorySystem, IPimSequencer and PimJob; C++17, as gem5's wrapper
  includes it), memory_system/impl/pim_dram_system.cpp (PimDRAMSystem,
  registered as PimDRAM: a copy of GenericDRAMSystem, which is final, plus
  the PIM entry points, the controllers' buffer views for gem5's held
  packets, and the resolved timing table and the
  sequencer's commands per class, PimTimingInfo, for gem5's closed-form
  atomic latency), src/ramulator/pim/ (pim_sequencer.cpp, the
  PimSequencer, registered as a controller plugin and used only as the
  tick hook, outside controller/plugin/: single-bank, paired, non-bank and
  all-bank jobs in the modes ideal, aim, baseline and aespa;
  pim_arb.h, the arbitration policies FR-FCFS, Chopim and AsyncDIMM
  (COSM stops with a message until it is defined); pim_unit.h;
  CMakeLists.txt), their
  generated Python wrappers,
  and the tests tests/controller_scheduling/pim_cases.py and
  test_pim_{ddr5,lpddr6,gddr7,hbm4}.py.
- tests/unit_tests/test_gem5_policies.py: decode test of the three ports
  through Gem5PortInterleave against a transcription of gem5's decode, on
  DDR4 and the four PIM standards' organizations, 1, 2 and 8 channels.

- SConscript: declares, only when the Kconfig option USE_RAMULATOR2 is
  set (build_opts/RISCV_RAMULATOR2), the files gem5 compiles into its
  binary: every *.cpp under src/ramulator/ except src/ramulator/python/
  (79 files at c6c7a5d), ext/yaml-cpp/src/*.cpp without contrib/ (30)
  and ext/fmt/src/{format,os}.cc (2), all as C++20 with the include
  paths and definitions upstream's CMake uses.
  src/mem/ramulator2/SConscript compiles them (gem5 runs ext/
  SConscripts before Source exists). The Ramulator 2.1 list is a glob;
  after an upstream update, check that it still equals the CMake lists,
  for example against compile_commands.json of a standalone build.
- ext/yaml-cpp and ext/fmt: the dependencies that CMakeLists.txt would
  fetch into exactly these directories (CMakeLists.txt:34-51), vendored
  so that no build needs the network. Copied with `git archive` from
  clean checkouts at the commits above, trimmed to LICENSE, README.md,
  include/, src/, CMakeLists.txt and the files their CMakeLists.txt
  reads (yaml-cpp: cmake_uninstall.cmake.in, yaml-cpp-config.cmake.in,
  yaml-cpp.pc.in; fmt: ChangeLog.md, support/cmake/). Dropped: tests,
  docs, Bazel and CI files, yaml-cpp's util/ and the rest of
  fmt's support/. Their tests and docs are off when they are built as
  subprojects, so the trimmed copies configure as is.
- ext/nanobind: nanobind v2.4.0 (the tag CMakeLists.txt:55-67 fetches into
  this directory when RAMULATOR_PYTHON_BINDINGS=ON) with its submodule
  ext/robin_map, for Ramulator 2.1's own Python bindings and tests only;
  gem5 compiles nothing from it. Cloned once with
  `git clone --branch v2.4.0 --recurse-submodules`, exported with
  `git archive` and trimmed to LICENSE, README.md, CMakeLists.txt,
  cmake/, include/ and src/ (robin_map: LICENSE, README.md, include/).
  Dropped: docs, tests, CI files and robin_map's CMake files, which
  nanobind does not read when ext/robin_map/include exists
  (cmake/nanobind-config.cmake).

The gem5 wrapper
----------------

The wrapper gem5 builds is src/mem/ramulator2, not resources/gem5_wrappers
(which stays unchanged, as the upstream reference). It derives from
resources/gem5_wrappers at c6c7a5d plus the changes of an earlier
prototype (packet splitting, static latencies, no 13.12 s FS delay, one due time
per response, integer clock period, decoding from the range base,
atomic operations on the write path), and these changes:
- the unsent parts of a split packet count as outstanding, so a drain
  waits for them;
- a DRAM clock period that rounds to 0 ps is fatal; the period and the
  transaction size are printed once at start-up;
- static latencies default to 10 ns frontend + 10 ns backend, as
  MemCtrl;
- the statistics files are named per instance:
  <object name>.ramulator_stats.yaml and
  <object name>.ramulator_stats.<tick>.yaml;
- the receive functions are virtual; the upstream DPRINTFs are kept;
- Ramulator 2.1's statistics are also gem5 statistics (always on):
  <object>.memory_system.*, <object>.channel<i>.* and, if it has any,
  <object>.frontend.*, one value per numeric leaf, read from the same
  text as the tick-named YAML file at every dump.
Only the vector-port class (Ramulator2VectorPorts) is built; the
one-port class (Ramulator2) is not ported. So the upstream helper
ramulator.gem5.Memory fails in gem5, while ramulator.gem5.VectorPortMemory
still works. Neither checks the memory size against the devices; the
stdlib component gem5.components.memory.ramulator2 does.

On an upstream update, diff resources/gem5_wrappers against c6c7a5d and
port the changes to src/mem/ramulator2 by hand.

Notes
-----

- The nested .gitignore is upstream's (apart from change 2). Upstream's
  version ignored two files that upstream tracks, ext/.clang-format and
  .devcontainer/devcontainer.json; both are tracked here (added with
  `git add -f`). Since change 2, only the second one is still ignored
  (by `*.json`).
- nanobind (v2.4.0) is vendored (ext/nanobind, above). gem5 does not
  need it; only Ramulator 2.1's own Python bindings and tests do.

Building in gem5
----------------

  scons build/RISCV_RAMULATOR2/gem5.opt -j$(nproc)

build_opts/RISCV_RAMULATOR2 sets the Kconfig option USE_RAMULATOR2
(src/mem/ramulator2/Kconfig, default n); build/RISCV and build/ALL do not
compile anything from this directory. The Python package python/ramulator
is embedded in that binary, so gem5 configurations can `import ramulator`
without a path; gem5 does not use its nanobind module. A quick check of
the build is configs/example/gem5_library/riscv-ramulator2-hello.py.

Building Ramulator 2.1 on its own (offline)
-------------------------------------------

For upstream's own C++ build, outside gem5, in a scratch copy of this
directory so that the tree stays clean:

  cp -r ext/ramulator2 <scratch>/ramulator2
  cmake -S <scratch>/ramulator2 -B <scratch>/build -G Ninja \
      -DFETCHCONTENT_FULLY_DISCONNECTED=ON \
      -DRAMULATOR_PYTHON_BINDINGS=OFF
  ninja -C <scratch>/build

CMake then uses the vendored ext/yaml-cpp and ext/fmt and downloads
nothing. Ramulator 2.1's Python tests (tests/) need the nanobind modules:
configure the same way with -DRAMULATOR_PYTHON_BINDINGS=ON (and
-DPython_EXECUTABLE=<a Python 3.10+ with pytest>), which uses the vendored
ext/nanobind and still downloads nothing, then run, in the scratch copy,

  PYTHONPATH=python python3 -m pytest tests/device_timings \
      tests/controller_scheduling tests/unit_tests

That build also runs the code generator in the copy (CMakeLists.txt:86-111),
which is why it must not run in the tree.

Generated sources
-----------------

The DRAM standards in src/ramulator/dram/impl/*.cpp and the Python
packages' __init__.py files are generated from python/ramulator/dram/
and are committed, as upstream does. Nothing in gem5's build runs the
generator. After editing python/ramulator/dram/*.py, regenerate and
commit the result with the edit:

  cd ext/ramulator2/python && python3 -m ramulator codegen
  cd .. && clang-format -i src/ramulator/dram/impl/*.cpp

(the same two commands that upstream's CMake targets codegen and
codegen_format run when RAMULATOR_PYTHON_BINDINGS=ON).

Updating from upstream
----------------------

In a clone of the upstream repository:
  git diff c6c7a5d <new commit> > upstream.diff
In the gem5 root:
  git apply --3way --directory=ext/ramulator2 upstream.diff
Then update the commit above, and:
- check that the SConscript's glob still equals upstream's CMake source
  lists (for example against compile_commands.json of a standalone
  build);
- diff resources/gem5_wrappers against c6c7a5d and port the changes to
  src/mem/ramulator2 by hand;
- update the lists of local changes and files above;
- rebuild build/RISCV_RAMULATOR2, run
  configs/example/gem5_library/riscv-ramulator2-hello.py, and compare the
  statistics of runs taken before the update with
  util/ramulator2_stats_diff.py (an upstream update that changes timing
  needs new reference runs).
